Thought: To determine the SMILES notation of the organic molecule, I should start by gathering some initial data about the molecule. The mass spectrometry spectra will provide the molecular weight and possible fragments, which can help in deducing the molecular formula. This is a good starting point for identifying the molecule.
Action: mass_spectrometry_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The mass spectrometry data indicates a molecular ion peak at m/z 179.0946, which suggests the molecular weight of the compound is approximately 179. This information can be used to deduce the molecular formula. Next, I will use the molecular weight to obtain possible molecular formulas and their isomers.
Action: obtain_isomers_from_molecular_formula
Action Input: {"molecular_formula": "C10H13NO2"}

Thought: I have obtained several possible isomers for the molecular formula C10H13NO2. To narrow down the correct structure, I should analyze the NMR spectra, starting with the proton NMR spectra, to identify the functional groups and connectivity in the molecule.
Action: proton_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Since the proton NMR prediction failed, I will try to gather information from the carbon NMR spectra, which can also provide valuable insights into the structure of the molecule.
Action: carbon_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: Both the proton and carbon NMR predictions have failed, which limits the direct structural information I can obtain from these spectra. I will now use the IR spectra to identify functional groups present in the molecule, which can help in narrowing down the possible isomers.
Action: ir_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: With the failure of IR, proton NMR, and carbon NMR predictions, I am limited in the direct experimental data I can use to deduce the structure. However, I can still use the molecular formula and the list of possible isomers to make an educated guess. I will validate each SMILES string from the list of isomers to ensure they are correct representations of the molecular formula C10H13NO2.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "[2H]C1=C(C(=C(C(=C1[2H])[2H])C[C@@H](C(=O)OC)N)[2H])[2H]"}

Thought: The first SMILES string is valid. I will proceed to validate the remaining SMILES strings to ensure they are all correct representations of the molecular formula C10H13NO2.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC1=C(C=CC(=C1)O)/C(=N\\OC)/C"}

Thought: The second SMILES string is also valid. I will continue validating the remaining SMILES strings.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CC(CC1=CC(=C(C=C1)N)O)C=O"}

Thought: The third SMILES string is valid. I will proceed to validate the next SMILES string.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "C#CC1CC2(C1)CC(C2)NC(=O)O"}

Thought: The fourth SMILES string is valid. I will now validate the final SMILES string from the list of isomers.
Action: validate_smiles
Action Input: {"smiles": "CCOC(=O)C1=C2CCCN2C=C1"}
